# Open Source Contribution Quality

Use this rule for all contributor-facing changes.
Be strict on reasoning quality and evidence discipline, flexible on output form.

## Contribution Quality Minimums

Each proposed content change should include:

- problem statement or motivation,
- intended audience,
- expected outcome,
- and definition of done.

## Clarity and Readability

- Use direct, plain language.
- Prefer active voice and concrete verbs.
- Avoid unexplained internal shorthand.
- Keep examples realistic and reproducible.

## Structural Expectations

- Place content in the correct playbook section.
- Cross-link related pages when introducing a new concept.
- Update contributor docs when adding new writing conventions.
- Allow adaptive section structure when it improves clarity and reasoning quality.

## Change Integrity

- Do not silently redefine core methodology terms.
- Highlight breaking semantic changes in PR description.
- Keep edits scoped; avoid unrelated rewrites.

## Reviewability

Contributors should make it easy for reviewers to verify:

- what changed,
- why it changed,
- and which CNS contract or methodology rule supports the change.
